.TH GIT-OPEN-BRANCH 1 "August 2026" "Noble Factor" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
git-open-branch \- open a branch and its worktree for a GitHub issue
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git-open-branch
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help ]
.RB [ \-i ]
.I issue
.RB [ \-\-name
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR target ]
.RB [ \-T
.IR type ]
.RB [ \-W | \-\-no\-worktree ]
.RB [ \-n | \-\-dry\-run ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git-open-branch
is the inverse of
.BR git-close-branch (1).
It creates a branch, and by default a linked worktree to hold it, from an issue.
.PP
.B An issue is required.
A branch names work, work is tracked, and an untracked branch is a claim nobody
can find later. The command reads the issue before it creates anything, so a
number that names nothing, or names finished work, is refused rather than
committed to a branch name.
.PP
The command performs the following steps:
.IP 1. 3
Reads the issue's title, state, and labels in a single
.BR gh (1)
call
.IP 2. 3
Derives the branch type from the labels, unless
.B \-\-type
names one
.IP 3. 3
Derives the readable half of the branch name from the title, unless
.B \-\-name
supplies one
.IP 4. 3
Fetches from origin, then evaluates every refusal
.IP 5. 3
Creates the branch from
.IR origin/<target> ,
in a new worktree unless
.B \-\-no\-worktree
was given
.SH NAMING
The branch is
.IR type / issue - name .
The worktree is the main worktree's path with
.RI . issue - name
appended \(em a sibling directory, the type prefix dropped, because a directory
listing is read by someone who already knows what the work is and needs to tell
one worktree from another.
.PP
Organization ruleset 12515090 constrains every ref to
.PP
.nf
^(chore|doc|feature|fix|hotfix|refactor|release)/.+$
.fi
.PP
with no bypass actors, so the prefix is not a preference. A name outside that
alternation is rejected at push time, which is a long way from here, and the
mapping from labels exists to make that impossible rather than merely unlikely:
.PP
.TS
tab(;);
l l.
\fBLabel\fR;\fBType\fR
bug;fix
documentation;doc
enhancement, feature, epic;feature
anything else;feature
.TE
.PP
An issue carrying none of these is work whose kind nobody has decided, and
.B feature
is the honest default rather than a guess dressed up.
.PP
The name derived from a title is lowercased, reduced to alphanumerics and
hyphens, stripped of a leading article, and truncated at 32 characters \(em at a
.B word
boundary, because a name ending in "remainin" is less recognizable than a
shorter one ending where a word does. The issue number carries the identity; the
name only has to be recognizable. A title is written to be read in a list of
issues rather than typed at a shell, so when the derivation is awkward,
.B \-\-name
says what to call the work instead.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage information and exit.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-issue " " \fIissue\fR
The issue this branch resolves. Equivalent to naming it as the operand, which is
the usual form: the flag exists so the operand has a name to be documented by. A
leading
.B #
is accepted and stripped.
.TP
.BI \-\-name " " name
The readable half of the branch name, replacing the one derived from the title.
Normalized the same way, so it may be written as prose. No short form: the long
name is typed rarely and reads clearly at the point of use.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-target " " \fIbranch\fR
The branch to branch from. Defaults to the repository's default branch, resolved
through
.IR origin/HEAD .
.TP
.BR \-T ", " \-\-type " " \fItype\fR
The branch prefix, overriding the one derived from the labels. Must be one of
.BR chore ,
.BR doc ,
.BR feature ,
.BR fix ,
.BR hotfix ,
.BR refactor ,
or
.BR release .
.TP
.BR \-W ", " \-\-no\-worktree
Create the branch in the current worktree with
.B git checkout \-b
instead of adding a linked worktree. Use it when the work is small enough that a
second directory is overhead.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Print the branch, the target, and the worktree that would be created \(em or the
refusal that would be raised \(em and exit. Changes nothing.
.SH REFUSALS
Four conditions stop the command, all evaluated after a fetch and all evaluated
.B before
the dry run branches, so the preview predicts them:
.IP \(bu 3
The branch already exists locally. Work it, or close it with
.BR git-close-branch (1).
.IP \(bu 3
The branch exists on origin. Fetch and check it out rather than opening a second
one.
.IP \(bu 3
There is no
.I origin/<target>
to branch from.
.IP \(bu 3
The worktree path already exists. Remove it, or work in it as it stands.
.PP
The fetch comes first because both branch signals read remote\-tracking
refs, and a stale ref produces a confident wrong answer.
.BR git-close-branch (1)
states the rule this follows: a preview that disagrees with the run it previews
is worse than no preview.
.PP
A closed issue is refused separately, and earlier. It is the interesting
refusal, because the mistake is nearly always a transposed number.
.SH SAFETY
.B git worktree add
touches no other worktree: no checkout elsewhere, no index change, no stash.
Whatever the caller is standing in is left exactly as it was found, uncommitted
changes and all. That is the property that makes opening a branch safe while
another is mid\-edit.
.PP
.B \-\-no\-worktree
does not share it.
.B git checkout \-b
switches the worktree the caller is standing in.
.PP
A checkout that fails removes both the worktree and the branch. Git creates the
branch before it writes any file, so a half\-made worktree would otherwise leave
a branch behind and the retry would refuse it as already existing. The branch is
deleted with
.B \-d
rather than
.BR \-D :
it was created moments earlier at
.I origin/<target>
and carries nothing, so a refusal means something else has committed to it, and
that is a branch worth keeping.
.SH ENCRYPTED REPOSITORIES
.BR git-crypt (1)
reads its key from
.IR $GIT_DIR/git\-crypt/keys ,
and a linked worktree's
.I $GIT_DIR
is
.I .git/worktrees/<name>
rather than the common
.IR .git .
A worktree created the ordinary way therefore has no key, and its checkout dies
on the first encrypted file it meets \(em which, in a repository that encrypts
.IR *.env
or
.IR *.pem ,
is nearly always the first one.
.PP
This command creates the worktree with
.B \-\-no\-checkout
so that nothing is written yet, links the common key directory into the new
worktree's
.IR $GIT_DIR ,
and only then checks out. A repository that does not use
.B git\-crypt
has no such directory, and the checkout proceeds unchanged.
.SH THE FIRST PUSH
.B git worktree add \-b \fIbranch\fB origin/\fItarget\fR
sets the new branch's upstream to
.IR origin/<target> ,
not to itself. With
.B push.default=simple
a bare
.B git push
then refuses rather than pushing to the target \(em safe, and confusing enough
that the command prints the first push for you:
.PP
.nf
git \-C \fIworktree\fR push \-u origin \fIbranch\fR
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success \- the branch, and any worktree, was created.
.TP
.B 64
Usage error \- no issue, a non\-numeric issue, a closed issue, a type
outside the ruleset, an unrecognized option, or a refusal.
.TP
.B 65
Data error \- the issue has no title to derive a name from.
.TP
.B 66
Cannot open input \- no such issue in this repository.
.TP
.B 69
Unavailable \- the
.BR gh (1)
CLI is not installed.
.TP
.B 70
Internal error \- the branch could not be created.
.TP
.B 72
Critical file missing \- the main worktree could not be located.
.TP
.B 73
Cannot create \- the worktree could not be created.
.TP
.B 75
Temporary failure \- origin could not be fetched.
.SH EXAMPLES
Open a branch for issue 762, taking the type from its labels and the name from
its title:
.PP
.nf
git\-open\-branch 762
.fi
.PP
See what that would do, without doing it:
.PP
.nf
git\-open\-branch 762 \-\-dry\-run
.fi
.PP
Name the work yourself, when the title does not reduce well:
.PP
.nf
git\-open\-branch 762 \-\-name "writ lifecycle surface"
.fi
.PP
Open a hotfix off a release branch, in the current worktree:
.PP
.nf
git\-open\-branch 803 \-T hotfix \-t release/2.4 \-\-no\-worktree
.fi
.SH WORKFLOW
The life of a branch, opened at one end and closed at the other:
.IP 1. 3
File the issue
.IP 2. 3
Run: git\-open\-branch <issue>
.IP 3. 3
Work in the new worktree; push with \-u the first time
.IP 4. 3
Open the PR, and squash\-merge it
.IP 5. 3
Run: git\-close\-branch \-\-merged
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1),
.BR git-worktree (1),
.BR gh (1),
.BR git-close-branch (1),
.BR git-reset-branch (1)
